# ...but those four patterns are unanchored and match ANY path segment, so they
# also swallow ordinary source. A test named `adminShellTokens.test.js` was
# written, run, passed locally and was invisible to git — it would never have
# reached CI, and nothing would have said so. In a codebase with an auth system
# `authToken.js`, `refreshToken.js` and `useCredentials.js` are all names
# somebody will reach for.
#
# So: keep the broad catch for the data files it is actually aimed at (a stray
# `token.json`, `github-token.txt`, `aws-credentials`), and exempt source. The
# exemptions are by EXTENSION, not by directory, so a secret cannot sneak back
# in by being dropped somewhere with the right name — a `.json` or `.txt` or
# extensionless file called `token` is still ignored wherever it lands.
!*token*.js
!*token*.jsx
!*token*.ts
!*token*.tsx
!*token*.css
!*secret*.js
!*secret*.jsx
!*secret*.ts
!*secret*.tsx
!*credentials*.js
!*credentials*.jsx
!*credentials*.ts
!*credentials*.tsx

